{
  "raw": {
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/nuigrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=011948": {
      "title": "Established Professor",
      "description": "Closing Date: 22/10/2026 Established Professor of Tumour Immunology, Full time, Permanent Applications are invited for an appointment as Established Professor of Tumour Immunology at University of Galway. This is a permanent, full-time position based in the Discipline of Advanced Therapies, School of Medicine, College of Medicine and Health. The University of Galway is a research-intensive institution with a strong commitment to excellence in teaching and research. The University’s strategic research priorities include Regenerative Medicine and Advanced Therapies, within which this appointment will play a key leadership role. For informal enquiries, please contact Prof. Matthew Griffin, Email: matthew.griffin@universityofgalway.ie Phone: +353 (0)91 495436 Additional information on the School of Medicine is available at: https://www.universityofgalway.ie/medicine-nursing-and-health-sciences/ Information on the University's Strategic Plan is available at: Strategy | Straitéis 2025-30 - University of Galway Salary: €147,198 to €186,094 p.a (applicable to new entrants effective from January, 2011) This appointment will be made on the Established Professor scale in line with current Government pay policy and in accordance with the terms and conditions of the University’s Remuneration policy. QA321-Updated-PP-Remuneration.pdf (universityofgalway.ie) Closing date for receipt of applications is 17:00 (Irish Time) on 22nd October 2026. It will not be possible to consider applications received after the closing date. Garda vetting may apply. Appointments will be conditional on work authorisation validation. Further details are available at Employment permits For more information and Application Form please see website: Jobs - University of Galway Applications should be submitted online. Please see further information on how to apply here: E-Recruit - University of Galway and Guidelines for On-line Applications (universityofgalway.ie) Please note that appointment to posts advertised will be dependent upon University approval, together with the terms of the Employment Control Framework for the higher education sector. At the University of Galway, we celebrate diversity and believe that every candidate can bring unique perspectives, talents and approaches to our University community. We actively encourage applications from all backgrounds regardless of race, religion, ethnicity, gender, family status, civil status, membership of the Traveller community, sexual orientation, disability or age. We value flexibility and recognise that a work-life balance is essential.¿ We offer flexible working arrangements, including hybrid options, to accommodate the diverse needs of our staff. Our commitment to inclusivity extends beyond recruitment as we foster a friendly and supportive work environment, where all work styles are valued and respected. Join us in shaping a workplace that thrives on diversity, creativity and collaboration. University of Galway is an equal opportunities employer."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/nuigrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=011988": {
      "title": "Clinical Tutor",
      "description": "Closing Date: 06/11/2026 Clinical Tutor, Part time (0.4FTE) Specific purpose Contract, Mayo Medical Academy, (surgery and peri-operative medicine) The School of Medicine at the University of Galway are looking for a suitably qualified and experienced candidate for the role of Clinical Tutor in the Mayo Medical Academy for a specific purpose contract –Maternity leave cover commencing in January 2027 in the following discipline: • Clinical Tutor in surgery and peri-operative medicine – Part time - 0.4FTE You will join progressive and professional teams based in the Mayo Medical Academy and the University of Galway, School of Medicine. Learn more about University of Galway Teaching Hospitals The purpose of this post is to support the delivery of teaching to undergraduate medical students, course organisation and assessment of students’ competence. In addition, the post-holder will have a prominent role in research and administration. The post would be suitable for work towards a higher degree (MMedSci or MD). The post will be based in the Castlebar Campus, and the post-holder will carry out the duties of the post under the direction of the Head of the Academy or of an authorised senior member of the staff of the School. The post-holder will support the undergraduate teaching activities in the School of Medicine. They will work closely with similar post-holders in the disciplines of Psychiatry, Primary Care, Surgery and Obstetrics as well as with clinical academic colleagues at University of Galway to provide co-ordinated development and delivery of specialist clinical education in the newly revised undergraduate medical programme. The successful applicant will be expected to actively participate in research and be involved in the formation of research collaborations with other academic departments and clinical staff as appropriate. They will also be expected to be involved in supervision of research with undergraduate and postgraduate students. They will ideally have some research and clinical teaching experience. Support will be available, as appropriate, to aid in the development of specialist educational skills. A commitment to quality in teaching will be sought and an outline understanding of medical education theory and practice is essential. They will need to be able to work with colleagues from diverse disciplines. The successful applicant will be a part-time (0.4FTE) employee of the University of Galway and will be appointed at the appropriate point on the Clinical Tutor salary. They will be a registered medical practitioner who has completed their general professional training. Working at University of Galway School of Medicine We offer excellent career development opportunities and benefits for our Clinical Teaching staff, including: • Supervised hands-on experience in course design, delivery and assessment • Opportunity for professional development in medical education in the form of Diploma or Higher Degree in Medical Education and Health Care Simulation • Exposure and training in a variety of teaching and assessment methods • Opportunities to grow and develop a network in the medical education field • Excellent Opportunity to explore career opportunities in clinical education field • Access to facilities and research at University of Galway • Opportunity to pursue personal research interests in line with the College of Medicine and Health strategy • Continued Professional Development • Opportunity to maintain knowledge currency in your discipline • Access to mentoring and coaching from experienced academic staff in the College • Opportunity to work with international student and staff community Appointments will be conditional on work authorisation validation and registration with the Irish Medical Council where applicable. Further details are available at www.dbei.ie and www.medicalcouncil.ie Please note that unfortunately we would be unable to apply for a work permit for these part time roles due to the fact these positions are less than 20 hours per week. Further information regarding the minimum number of hours per week for an employment permit can be found here: - Employment Permits FAQs (enterprise.gov.ie) under section 5.6. Informal enquiries may be made to: Dr Michelle Duggan, Mayo Medical Academy; E-mail: michelle.duggan@universityofgalway.ie Queries in relation to the application process may be addressed to recruit@universityofgalway.ie Interviews are planned for Nov 2026 Salary: the appointment will be made on the Clinical Tutor 1 salary scale: € 70,276– € 82,756per annum or Clinical Tutor 2: € 83,847- € 102,619 pro rata per annum. The relevant salary scale will be determined based on candidate’s experience and in accordance with the terms and conditions of the University’s Remuneration policy.QA321-Updated-PP-Remuneration.pdf (This appointment will be made on the Clinical Tutor scale in line with current Government pay policy) Closing date for receipt of applications is 17:00 (Irish Time) on 6th November 2026. It will not be possible to consider applications received after the closing date. Garda vetting will apply. For more information and Application Form please see website: Jobs - University of Galway Applications should be submitted online. Please see further information on how to apply here: E-Recruit - University of Galway and Guidelines for On-line Applications (universityofgalway.ie) Please note that appointments to posts advertised will be dependent upon University approval, together with the terms of the Employment Control Framework for the higher education sector. University of Galway is an equal opportunities employer."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/nuigrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=011990": {
      "title": "Lecturer - Contract Type A",
      "description": "Closing Date: 09/11/2026 Lecturer in Medical Intern Education, part time (0.5FTE) Specific purpose contract Applications are invited for an appointment as Lecturer in Medical Intern Education, Part time at the University of Galway. The post is offered as a Part time 0.5FTE, Specific purpose contract- Maternity leave cover. The primary purpose of this post is to provide education and teaching leadership to the intern programme at Galway University Hospitals and the wider West North West Intern Network. Development of the education programme in line with best practice and changing requirements for clinical practice, is part of this role. There are approx. 150 interns spread across 9 hospital sites in the network and a number of GP practices in the region. The interns are students of the School of Medicine and employees of the HSE. The post holder will be expected to plan and contribute to the intern education program in the West North West Intern network. In addition to successful candidate will be expected to engage in quality improvement and scholarship of intern education. The post-holder will work as part of the team with the clinical intern coordinator and the grade 5 intern programme administrator. In addition, the post-holder will be situated in the discipline of medical education within the school of medicine. As such, the post holder will be supported in their educational activity by a community of fellow educators. In addition, they will contribute to education grants awarded to the School of Medicine for intern education and training. The School of Medicine is responsible for intern education and training across the West North West Intern network. The lecturer in medical intern education has responsibility for the interns affiliated to the University of Galway as they complete their 12-month internship and for planning and delivery of the education and training programme. The training requirements and responsibilities are outlined in a service level agreement that exists between the Health Service Executive National Doctors Training and Planning (HSE NDTP) and the School of Medicine, University of Galway. The post holder will coordinate, deliver and run the intern education and training programme as per the SLA. For informal enquiries, please contact Dr Laura Heffernan = laura.heffernan1@hse.ie Additional information on the School of Medicine is available at: School of Medicine - University of Galway Information on the University's Strategic Plan is available at: Strategy | Straitéis 2025-30 - University of Galway Salary: € 65,552 to € 104,573 pro rata p.a. (applicable to new entrants effective from January 2011)This appointment will be made on the Lecturer scale in line with current Government pay policy and in accordance with the terms and conditions of the University’s Remuneration policy. QA321-Updated-PP-Remuneration.pdf (universityofgalway.ie) Closing date for receipt of applications is 17:00 (Irish Time) on 9th November 2026. It will not be possible to consider applications received after the closing date. Garda vetting may apply. Appointments will be conditional on work authorisation validation. Further details are available at www.dbei.ie For more information and Application Form please see website: Jobs - University of Galway Applications should be submitted online. Please see further information on how to apply here: E-Recruit - University of Galway and Guidelines for online applications.pdf Please note that appointment to posts advertised will be dependent upon University approval, together with the terms of the Employment Control Framework for the higher education sector. University of Galway is an equal opportunities employer."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/nuigrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=012004": {
      "title": "Lecturer - Contract Type B",
      "description": "Lecturer in Health Promotion, Full-Time, Permanent, Contract Type B Lecturer in Health Promotion, Full-Time, Permanent, Contract Type B The Discipline of Health Promotion invites applications for a permanent, full-time (1FTE), Type B Lecturer position. The successful candidate will contribute to the Discipline’s research and teaching activities, including programme coordination, curriculum development and postgraduate research supervision. The Discipline of Health Promotion is a dynamic, multidisciplinary and research-active Discipline within the School of Allied and Community Health, with responsibility for a range of innovative teaching and research programmes at the University of Galway. The School of Allied and Community Health is committed to embedding equality, diversity and inclusion within its culture and holds a Bronze Athena SWAN Award. The Discipline of Health Promotion delivers and contributes to undergraduate and postgraduate programmes across the College of Medicine and Health and the College of Science and Engineering. All academic staff within the Discipline are research active through an active programme of national and international research through the Health Promotion Research Centre, which is designated as a World Health Organization (WHO) Collaborating Centre for Health Promotion Research. Candidates should hold a PhD in Health Promotion, Public Health, Epidemiology or a closely related field. The candidate should demonstrate a strong record of accomplishment in third-level teaching and relevant expertise in health promotion research. Candidates should also demonstrate experience in curriculum development and the capacity to contribute to academic leadership, programme coordination and postgraduate research supervision. As a Type B academic appointment, candidates should demonstrate an active research profile appropriate to their career stage, including a record of high-quality research outputs and engagement in collaborative research. Evidence of experience in applying and/or securing research funding and developing national and/or international research collaborations will be important. The successful candidate will contribute to teaching, programme coordination and curriculum development across programmes to which the Discipline contributes, with particular responsibilities in undergraduate medical and social care education and postgraduate Health Promotion programmes, including micro-credential programmes. The successful candidate will also be expected to develop and sustain an active programme of research aligned with the strategic priorities of the Discipline of Health Promotion and the Health Promotion Research Centre. This will include producing high-quality research outputs, contributing to external research funding activity, developing collaborative research at national and/or international levels, and supervising postgraduate and doctorate research students. The successful candidate will be expected to contribute to the wider academic and collegial activities of the Discipline, School, College and University, as appropriate. For informal enquiries, please contact Dr Mary Jo Lavelle, Head of Discipline of Health Promotion, Email: mary.lavelle@universityofgalway.ie. Additional information on the School of Allied and Community Health is available at: https://www.universityofgalway.ie/medicine-nursing-and-health-sciences/allied-community-health/. Salary: Lecturer salary scales start at €65,552 to €104,573 (applicable to new entrants effective from January, 2011). This appointment will be made on the Lecturer scale in line with current Government pay policy and in accordance with the terms and conditions of the University’s Remuneration policy. See: QA321-Remuneration-Payroll-Policy.pdf The full-time hours of work for academic staff are 39 hours per week pro rata. Information on the University's Strategic Plan is available at: Strategy | Straitéis 2025-30 - University of Galway Closing date for receipt of applications is 17:00 (Irish Time) on December 10th, 2026. It will not be possible to consider applications received after the closing date. Appointments will be conditional on work authorisation validation. Further details are available at Employment Permits - DETE Garda vetting may apply. For more information and Application Form please see website: Jobs - University of Galway Applications should be submitted online. Please see further information on how to apply here: E-Recruit - University of Galway and Guidelines for On-line Applications (universityofgalway.ie) Please note that appointment to posts advertised will be dependent upon University approval, together with the terms of the Employment Control Framework for the higher education sector. At the University of Galway, we celebrate diversity and believe that every candidate can bring unique perspectives, talents and approaches to our University community. We actively encourage applications from all backgrounds regardless of race, religion, ethnicity, gender, family status, civil status, membership of the Traveller community, sexual orientation, disability or age. We value flexibility and recognise that a work-life balance is essential. We offer flexible working arrangements, including hybrid options, to accommodate the diverse needs of our staff. Our commitment to inclusivity extends beyond recruitment as we foster a friendly and supportive work environment, where all work styles are valued and respected. Join us in shaping a workplace that thrives on diversity, creativity and collaboration. University of Galway is an equal opportunities employer."
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/nuigrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=012010": {
      "title": "Lecturer - Contract Type A",
      "description": "Closing Date: 26/11/2026 Lecturer in Preventive Cardiology, 1FTE, Contract type A, Specific Purpose Contract 012010 The School of Medicine at the University of Galway are looking for suitably qualified and experienced candidate for the post of Lecturer in Preventive Cardiology on a specific purpose contract for leave cover for a 1.0 FTE appointment. We are seeking to appoint a doctor to this post who will join the Discipline of Clinical Medicine and Population Health. The successful post holder will serve the educational needs of the Masters in Preventive Cardiology and will be expected to contribute to the planning and delivery of teaching, student support and assessment duties. You will join progressive and professional teams based in the University of Galway, School of Medicine.¿Learn more about University of Galway Teaching Hospitals Appointment will be conditional on work authorisation. Further details are available at www.dbei.ie Informal enquiries in relation to the position can be made to: Dr Andrea Holmes at andrea.a.holmes@universityofgalway.ie Queries in relation to the application process may be addressed to recruit@universityofgalway.ie Salary: Lecturer salary scales start at €65,552 to 104,573 (applicable to new entrants effective from January, 2011).This appointment will be made on the Lecturer scale in line with current Government pay policy and in accordance with the terms and conditions of the University’s Remuneration policy. See: QA321-Remuneration-Payroll-Policy.pdf Information on the University's Strategic Plan is available at: Strategy | Straitéis 2025-30 - University of Galway Closing date for receipt of applications is 17:00 (Irish Time) on 26th November 2026. It will not be possible to consider applications received after the closing date. Appointments will be conditional on work authorisation validation. Further details are available at Employment Permits - DETE Garda vetting may apply. For more information and Application Form please see website: Jobs - University of Galway Applications should be submitted online. Please see further information on how to apply here: E-Recruit - University of Galway and Guidelines for On-line Applications (universityofgalway.ie) Please note that appointment to posts advertised will be dependent upon University approval, together with the terms of the Employment Control Framework for the higher education sector. At the University of Galway, we celebrate diversity and believe that every candidate can bring unique perspectives, talents and approaches to our University community. We actively encourage applications from all backgrounds regardless of race, religion, ethnicity, gender, family status, civil status, membership of the Traveller community, sexual orientation, disability or age. We value flexibility and recognise that a work-life balance is essential. We offer flexible working arrangements, including hybrid options, to accommodate the diverse needs of our staff. Our commitment to inclusivity extends beyond recruitment as we foster a friendly and supportive work environment, where all work styles are valued and respected. Join us in shaping a workplace that thrives on diversity, creativity and collaboration. University of Galway is an equal opportunities employer."
    }
